/// IoDevice — an opaque `file:io_device()` handle.
///
/// Erlang's `file:open/2` answers an io_device (a file-server pid or a raw
/// file descriptor) and `file:close/1`, `file:sendfile/…` and friends take it
/// back. The checker types those FFI results with this class so the handle
/// stays a named type (rather than `Dynamic`).
///
/// An IoDevice has no protocol of its own: its representation is not
/// inspectable, and sending it any message other than the `Object` protocol
/// (identity and equality such as `=:=`) is a type error. For ordinary file
/// work use `File` and `FileHandle`.
///
/// This is an opaque-handle class in the "Object" kind of the three-kind
/// model (ADR 0067): no `state:`/`field:` slots and not instantiable from
/// Beamtalk. It is declared only so FFI specs have a type to name.
///
/// The class is static only: at run time the value is a Pid (or a Tuple for a raw descriptor), so `x class` answers
/// that and `x isKindOf:` against this class is always false.
/// Process-scoped (ADR 0103): a raw descriptor is usable only by the process
/// that opened it, so `#process` is the conservative bound for the whole
/// class — the same approximation `FileHandle` makes, even though the
/// io_server-pid case would survive the hop.
sealed typed Object subclass: IoDevice
  handleScope: #process
